# TARGET FILE: .env.example  (repo root — C:/Users/Nicholas_N/Desktop/claude code/signaldeck/.env.example)
#
# HOW TO APPLY (from the repo root):
#     git apply --check drafts/patches/env-example-token-leak.patch   # dry run
#     git apply         drafts/patches/env-example-token-leak.patch
#   or, without git:
#     patch -p1 < drafts/patches/env-example-token-leak.patch
#
# WHY (finding: HIGH — .env.example:17-18 instructs the operator to leak the
# admin bearer token into the client bundle):
#
#   The current text reads, verbatim at .env.example:16-19 —
#       # Optional bearer token — set this to require auth on EVERY API request
#       # (needed before exposing the daemon beyond localhost). Web app reads
#       # NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNALDECK_TOKEN to match it.
#       # SIGNALDECK_API_TOKEN=
#
#   The offending VARIABLE NAME is NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNALDECK_TOKEN.
#
#   A bearer token can NEVER be a NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable. Next.js does not read
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_* at runtime on the client — it TEXTUALLY SUBSTITUTES the value
#   into the JavaScript bundle during `next build`. The literal secret is then
#   served to every browser that loads the site, permanently, in a static asset
#   that CDNs and browser caches keep. It is visible in view-source and in
#   devtools with no tooling. Rotating the token requires a rebuild + redeploy,
#   and every copy already handed out stays valid until the daemon-side value
#   changes. There is no runtime guard, lint rule or CSP that prevents this;
#   the leak happens silently at build time.
#
#   The blast radius here is total rather than partial. In the daemon,
#   resolveUser() falls back from the session cookie to the bearer token, and
#   that bearer maps to the ADMIN user (documented in
#   web/src/app/api/[...path]/route.ts:49-57). So an inlined token does not
#   merely grant read access — it grants every visitor admin: mutating the
#   admin watchlist/portfolio, burning the LLM daily budget, and defeating
#   SIGNALDECK_PUBLIC_READS=false.
#
# CURRENT EXPOSURE STATUS — DOCUMENTATION-ONLY, NOT LIVE.
#   Verified: no file under web/src reads NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNALDECK_TOKEN.
#     $ grep -rn "NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNALDECK_TOKEN" web/src   ->  no matches
#     $ grep -rn "NEXT_PUBLIC" web/src
#         web/src/app/api/[...path]/route.ts:4  (a comment: "never NEXT_PUBLIC")
#         web/src/lib/api.ts:6,8                (NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNALDECK_API — a
#                                                base URL, not a credential)
#   The client-side read existed historically (commit 7bd158f added it under
#   web/; a601b3c removed it) and the code was migrated to the server-side
#   proxy. Only the root .env.example was never updated, so it is now stale
#   guidance that directly contradicts the correct instruction already present
#   at web/.env.example:8 ("Server-only — never expose this as NEXT_PUBLIC_*").
#
#   Severity is therefore "an operator following the shipped instructions
#   creates a total-compromise leak", not "a leak is live today". It stays HIGH
#   because the instruction is the only guidance an operator has at the moment
#   they enable auth, and following it produces an unrecoverable, cache-
#   persisted admin credential disclosure.
#
# NOT FIXED BY THIS PATCH (out of scope, flagged for the orchestrator):
#   The same stale text is present in 8 checked-out worktrees under
#   .claude/worktrees/*/.env.example:18. Those are separate working copies;
#   they inherit the fix when they rebase onto a branch carrying it.
#
diff --git a/.env.example b/.env.example
index 4641d86..d9b0e8c 100644
--- a/.env.example
+++ b/.env.example
@@ -14,8 +14,17 @@
 # Hard daily cap on total LLM calls (spend guard; pooled across keys). Default 2000.
 # SIGNALDECK_LLM_DAILY_CAP=2000
 # Optional bearer token — set this to require auth on EVERY API request
-# (needed before exposing the daemon beyond localhost). Web app reads
-# NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNALDECK_TOKEN to match it.
+# (needed before exposing the daemon beyond localhost). SERVER-ONLY SECRET.
+# To let the web app reach a token-protected daemon, put the SAME value in
+# web/.env.local as SIGNALDECK_API_TOKEN (see web/.env.example) — the Next.js
+# /api/* proxy route reads it server-side and it never reaches the browser.
+# NEVER name it NEXT_PUBLIC_*: Next.js substitutes every NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable
+# into the client JS bundle at BUILD time, so the literal value ships to every
+# visitor and is readable in devtools/view-source. There is no runtime check
+# that catches this — the build simply inlines it. This token maps to the
+# ADMIN user in the daemon's resolveUser(), so publishing it hands any visitor
+# full admin. Browsers authenticate with the HttpOnly session cookie instead;
+# the bearer exists for non-browser clients that call the daemon directly.
 # SIGNALDECK_API_TOKEN=
 # Comma-separated CORS-allowed web origins (defaults cover localhost:8323/3000).
 # SIGNALDECK_WEB_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8323
